{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1795375994,"nonce":"VEA+OHgGv5qS4EenDXzK2LE8cWs=","title":"Immix mark is slow on long lists of small nodes","message":"The mark phase of the Immix heap takes 3 to 8 ms for each MB it reaches on lists of 16-byte nodes. That is 55 to 130 ns for each node. Stop and sweep together take about 3% of a collection, so marking sets the cost. The multi-thread path is not the cause: one thread shows the same rate. Related: 3bbb229 (pacing) and 5c7208a (the heap).\n\nReproducer. Build the example with `cargo build --release -p zyntax_compiler --example threaded_rows`. Then run `ZYNTAX_HOST_HEAP=immix ZYNTAX_IMMIX_TRACE=1 target/release/examples/threaded_rows retain1`. The row `retain` runs the same work on four threads.\n\nNumbers, release build on the x86 NUC. Each thread builds a list of 300000 nodes and frees 8 blocks of 64 bytes for each node.\n\n- retain1, one thread: 4 collections. Mark takes 3.1 ms at 994 KB reached and 14.0 ms at 3975 KB. Wall time is 0.105 s.\n- retain, four threads: 17 collections. Mark takes 129 ms at 16.9 MB reached, which is 7.6 ms for each MB. Wall time is 1.7 s, and 1.2 s of that is mark.\n- The pool, which frees each block at once, takes 0.028 s on the same row.\n\nThe cost for each MB doubles when four lists are live. Cache misses on the side tables are the likely cause. A collection runs for every 32 MB of allocation while the live set stays small. So the total mark work is the number of collections times the live size, and it grows fast with live data.\n\nIdeas to try: prefetch the next node before the table lookups, mark in batches from a stack, and test a word against the heap range before any lookup. Measure each one on retain1 first.","files":null}]}